00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:04.700
got me started on a life of crime, which is what my mother thought my youthful interest in jazz

00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:14.490
implied, was a case of the measles when I was 15. In those days, the early 1930s, when you had the

00:01:14.490 --> 00:01:19.640
measles, they put you in a darkened room and you were not allowed to read. And so my mother let me

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:25.000
have the old family radio. And I lay there wide awake in the night, picking up those strange

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:29.390
sounds in the night. Duke Ellington, Louis Armstrong.

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:37.190
Cab Calloway, Earl Hines, Fletcher Henderson, and all I knew was what I heard. And what I heard

00:01:37.190 --> 00:01:43.900
gave a thrust to my life which has never left it. I have learned more from them than I did in any

00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:50.810
classroom, and their art has given me a faith in creativity and in life itself that no pulpit has

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:59.560
ever offered. For millions of readers in the 1950s, 60s, and 70s, Ralph J. Gleason's personal jazz

00:01:59.560 --> 00:01:59.860
is common.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:04.920
were a powerful introduction to America's most vibrant musical scene,

00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:08.620
a way of appreciating a challenging new art form

00:02:08.620 --> 00:02:11.560
through the affectionate eyes of a true disciple.

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:14.700
Co-founder of the Monterey Jazz Festival,

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:17.470
co-founder of Rolling Stone Magazine,

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:20.960
contributing writer and editor of Downbeat Magazine,

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:24.440
the world's first syndicated jazz columnist

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:27.620
at the San Francisco Chronicle for over a quarter century,

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:29.900
Gleason's literary exploration

00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:37.000
of jazz had only one purpose. He wanted people to dig the music. The thing that a modern jazz

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:43.040
musician does, which you should really keep in mind when you see him in concerts or see him in

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:49.980
jazz clubs, is somewhat similar to looking at a poet standing in the middle of a supermarket and

00:02:50.330 --> 00:02:57.490
improvising poetry. They are called upon by the discipline of this art form to go into public

00:02:57.490 --> 00:02:59.530
places and to spontaneously

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:22.480
create music unlike a poet unlike the writer of a novel unlike a painter they

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:28.150
have no opportunity to take this product that they have created and reform it and

00:03:28.150 --> 00:03:29.930
correct the mistakes that they might have

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:32.120
or change the way in which they approach it.

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:35.340
What they do is done for all time right then when they do it.

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:42.320
This is a very unique thing about jazz,

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:46.160
and it's one of the things that gives it a particular quality of aliveness

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:50.260
that makes it one of the most interesting and vital of all contemporary art forms.

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:56.020
In 1959, Gleason took the unprecedented step of ushering jazz

00:03:56.020 --> 00:03:58.100
from smoky late night clubs

00:03:58.100 --> 00:03:59.900
to the blue-gray flickerings

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:01.780
of early television broadcasting,

00:04:02.380 --> 00:04:05.580
creating, producing, and hosting Jazz Casual,

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:08.360
the first nationally televised series

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:10.260
devoted entirely to jazz.

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:14.030
On a stage left intentionally barren,

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:17.620
Gleason invited his friends to fill the studio space

00:04:17.620 --> 00:04:20.700
with spontaneous innovations of pure jazz,

00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:24.220
and his friends conjured up a special magic.

00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:29.820
John Coltrane offered his only television

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:36.880
in North America. Count Basie visited on his own birthday, improvising with a

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:45.150
special hand-picked quartet. Artists agreed to appear on Jazz Casual who

00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:50.000
would have otherwise avoided the camera's glare, because Gleason allowed them the

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:54.900
freedom to play what they wanted for as long as they wanted, mixing the music

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:56.620
with insight and celebration.

00:05:04.220 --> 00:05:12.020
There are very few people that I consider qualified to judge whether someone is a jazz player or a jazz singer.

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:15.740
Now, you happen to be one of the few people that I feel is fully qualified.

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:17.340
You could cancel off the show for that.

00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:18.440
Am I a jazz singer?

00:05:19.140 --> 00:05:19.540
No.

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:21.140
Am I a jazz singer?

00:05:21.160 --> 00:05:24.550
You are to me, but I've got a very good friend who thinks you aren't.

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:27.180
When Sonny gets...

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:59.560
sigh of sadness like the wind that stirs the tree you'd like to buy my way what better definition

00:06:00.540 --> 00:06:04.340
a jazz singer could we possibly have than Carmen McRae.

00:06:09.610 --> 00:06:12.570
Earl Father Hines is really the father of jazz piano,

00:06:13.780 --> 00:06:16.140
the most important musicians who has ever played this music,

00:06:16.520 --> 00:06:19.270
and today you really can't play jazz piano

00:06:19.270 --> 00:06:21.840
without playing a little bit of Earl Father Hines.

00:06:22.340 --> 00:06:23.760
Thanks for that wonderful introduction.

00:06:24.120 --> 00:06:27.000
Well, you know, I mean it, and you deserve it,

00:06:27.080 --> 00:06:27.940
and we all know this.

