00:15:41.320 --> 00:15:43.420
This is Jazz Casual, and I'm Ralph Gleason.

00:15:43.730 --> 00:15:46.110
You've been listening to the John Coltrane Quartet,

00:15:46.480 --> 00:15:50.240
playing a composition by Mongo Santa Maria called Afro Blue.

00:15:51.080 --> 00:15:53.660
John Coltrane, like many of the modern jazz musicians,

00:15:53.960 --> 00:15:59.340
feels that the music itself speaks far more fluently than any human ever could.

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:04.960
I agree with that thoroughly, and I also endorse the position of Miles Davis that albums

00:16:04.960 --> 00:16:07.140
shouldn't have liner notes, even when I write them.

00:16:08.220 --> 00:16:13.440
The thing that a modern jazz musician does, and which you should really keep in mind when

00:16:13.440 --> 00:16:19.980
you see him in concerts or see him in jazz clubs, is somewhat similar to looking at a

00:16:19.980 --> 00:16:24.260
poet standing in the middle of a supermarket and improvising poetry.

00:16:24.260 --> 00:16:29.670
They are called upon by the discipline of this art form to...

00:16:30.030 --> 00:16:36.790
go into public places where people are gathered informally and to spontaneously create music.

00:16:37.510 --> 00:16:44.250
Unlike a poet, unlike the writer of a novel, unlike a painter, they have no opportunity

00:16:44.250 --> 00:16:50.420
to take this product that they have created and reform it and correct the mistakes that

00:16:50.420 --> 00:16:53.000
they might have made or change the way in which they approach it.

00:16:53.060 --> 00:16:56.220
What they do is done for all time right then when they do it.

00:16:56.220 --> 00:16:59.970
This is a very unique thing about jazz and it's one of the things that gives it

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:04.840
a particular quality of aliveness that makes it one of the most interesting and vital of

00:17:04.840 --> 00:17:06.520
all contemporary art forms.

